{"pdfpcFormat":2,"duration":12,"disableMarkdown":false,"noteFontSize":20,"pages":[{"idx":0,"label":"1","overlay":0,"note":"Review of three CFD solvers submitted in fulfillment of the HIPC module assessment.\n"},{"idx":1,"label":"2","overlay":0,"note":"Start by surveying problem.  We want to numerically solve for velocity vectors and pressure scalars around a body specified as an N A C A airfoil."},{"idx":2,"label":"2","overlay":1,"note":"This runs over time.  At each step, the SW executes a series of sub-routines to improve the state of the simulation."},{"idx":3,"label":"2","overlay":2,"note":"First we apply boundary conditions to the velocity vector field to fix numerical stability.  We then compute tentative velocities and a forcing term for the pressure equation, which we refer to as the Poisson forcing term.  Successive over-relaxation is then used to solve the Poisson partial differential equation for the pressure, and we finally use the tentative values and pressure scalar field to derive the velocities at the fixed time-step."},{"idx":4,"label":"2","overlay":3,"note":"First we apply boundary conditions to the velocity vector field to fix numerical stability.  We then compute tentative velocities and a forcing term for the pressure equation, which we refer to as the Poisson forcing term.  Successive over-relaxation is then used to solve the Poisson partial differential equation for the pressure, and we finally use the tentative values and pressure scalar field to derive the velocities at the fixed time-step."},{"idx":5,"label":"2","overlay":4,"note":"First we apply boundary conditions to the velocity vector field to fix numerical stability.  We then compute tentative velocities and a forcing term for the pressure equation, which we refer to as the Poisson forcing term.  Successive over-relaxation is then used to solve the Poisson partial differential equation for the pressure, and we finally use the tentative values and pressure scalar field to derive the velocities at the fixed time-step."},{"idx":6,"label":"2","overlay":5,"note":"First we apply boundary conditions to the velocity vector field to fix numerical stability.  We then compute tentative velocities and a forcing term for the pressure equation, which we refer to as the Poisson forcing term.  Successive over-relaxation is then used to solve the Poisson partial differential equation for the pressure, and we finally use the tentative values and pressure scalar field to derive the velocities at the fixed time-step."},{"idx":7,"label":"2","overlay":6,"note":"First we apply boundary conditions to the velocity vector field to fix numerical stability.  We then compute tentative velocities and a forcing term for the pressure equation, which we refer to as the Poisson forcing term.  Successive over-relaxation is then used to solve the Poisson partial differential equation for the pressure, and we finally use the tentative values and pressure scalar field to derive the velocities at the fixed time-step."},{"idx":8,"label":"2","overlay":7,"note":"Most of these sub-routines are embarrassingly parallel; that is to say that they are likely to benefit substantially from parallelization.  In this presentation will provide a review of three approaches and their observed performance gains."},{"idx":9,"label":"3","overlay":0,"note":"We first discuss OpenMP; an abstraction layer of threading and a shared memory pool to provide parallelism requiring only a minimal number of modifications to the serial counterpart."},{"idx":12,"label":"3","overlay":3,"note":"Profiling indicated that solving of the Poisson PDE took a majority of runtime in most cases.  Intel Advisor showed that the serial implementation could not be automatically vectorised, so the algorithm was refactored to reduce branching and pack and pre-load neighbouring values into the AVX2 SIMD registers."},{"idx":15,"label":"3","overlay":6,"note":"Where the Advisor and Intel VTune identified additional scalar loops without data dependencies, the OpenMP API was used to parallelise iterations over the two-dimensional scalar and vector fields."},{"idx":16,"label":"4","overlay":0,"note":"These two images from the Advisor display profiling results for the scalar and auto-vectorised loops in the solver, retrieved from the serial and OpenMP implementations respectively."},{"idx":17,"label":"5","overlay":0,"note":"Further, this code sample displays a simplified usage of OpenMP to update the velocity vector field, in which a single parallel region is created to initialise a hot thread pool, which is then used by two OpenMP parallelised loops marked with the ‘nowait’ keyword to maximise data throughput.  The asynchronous capabilities of OpenMP were used wherever data dependencies were absent; note in this example, the end of the outer parallel region is an implicit barrier."},{"idx":18,"label":"6","overlay":0,"note":"We now move to the multi-core parallelisation approach using the standardised Message Passing Interface, or MPI.  The MPI situation requires a more complex and nuanced approach as we don’t have shared memory; each node manages its own memory space and explicitly passes messages between instances of the running application."},{"idx":19,"label":"6","overlay":1,"note":"Using the approach from Griebel, the two-dimensional domain is decomposed into roughly equivalently sized subdomains, with the exact sizes determined at runtime according to number of compute resources available."},{"idx":20,"label":"6","overlay":2,"note":"Notably, subdomains overlap by a single unit on each axis to accommodate the extremities of neighbouring regions; these are known as ghost rows and ghost columns, and are populated with halo exchanges implemented by message-passing."},{"idx":22,"label":"6","overlay":4,"note":"Each subdomain is also marked by a series of flags to indicate its role within the overall two-dimensional Cartesian virtual topology established by MPI, such as whether the region is on a boundary or contains ghost cells in a particular direction."},{"idx":23,"label":"6","overlay":5,"note":"As with the OpenMP port, non-blocking communication is used wherever possible.  Where there is a natural barrier, OpenMPI provides a blocking call to ensure data integrity."},{"idx":24,"label":"7","overlay":0,"note":"In this next code sample, we can see an asynchronous OpenMPI reduction over a vector field to produce a heuristic, in which velocities in the X and Y directions are maximised and broadcast back to all nodes in the cluster."},{"idx":25,"label":"8","overlay":0,"note":"Finally we review the NVIDIA CUDA strategy. "},{"idx":26,"label":"8","overlay":1,"note":"This doesn’t require as explicit domain decomposition as memory management is largely abstracted by the API, but we still do pay attention to mapping to preserve memory coalescing and ensure that we’re not spending excessive cycles on retrieving memory from threads outside of the warp."},{"idx":28,"label":"8","overlay":3,"note":"We have a column-major memory layout that is indexed at constant strides and correctly aligned with the align keyword provided by CUDA.  Reductions can be implemented with a two-stage mechanism, first occurring on a single thread block, and then across the entire device, using an approach introduced by the referenced 2007 presentation by Mark Harris at NVIDIA."},{"idx":29,"label":"8","overlay":4,"note":"Similar to OpenMPI, CUDA exposes a runtime API call to determine the optimal block size according to the problem size and available compute resources."},{"idx":30,"label":"9","overlay":0,"note":"Here is a further code sample demonstrating the shared-memory reduction for a templated type. In this case, we fold elements into a sum and explicitly synchronise the threads to distribute the result across blocks, and write the sum back into the device memory that can later be copied to the host."},{"idx":31,"label":"10","overlay":0,"note":"To validate the correctness, each port contained a VTK serialiser, the results of which are viewable in any number of scientific data visualisation applications such as VisIt or ParaView."},{"idx":32,"label":"10","overlay":1,"note":"In the OpenMP and CUDA cases, the dataset was collected on the host (on a single node or single thread or whichever was applicable) and written to a single file."},{"idx":33,"label":"10","overlay":2,"note":"MPI could make use of the parallel capability of the VTK format whereby each node wrote a separate file representing its own subdomain, which can be stitched together by the viewing application with a metadata file."},{"idx":35,"label":"10","overlay":4,"note":"This allows us to avoid the complexities and annoyances of parallel I/O, and makes use of a well-supported feature of the VTK format.\n"},{"idx":36,"label":"11","overlay":0,"note":"Here we can see two samples of the VTK results: a velocity vector field, or the magnitude thereof, and a pressure scalar field over the same domain after two seconds of simulation time.  I only show one set of results here as all results were all essentially identical, except some minor deviations due to differences in floating-point arithmetic; this can be explained by the fast-math optimisations which can violate strict compliance with IEEE-754 to achieve use of more performant CPU instructions.  But on the whole it’s what we’d expect."},{"idx":40,"label":"12","overlay":3,"note":"Onto some performance analysis and the results of profiling, we executed all test cases on an exclusive node on the Viking HPC cluster.  To give a very quick overview, a single node as a 48-core AMD EPYC processors on two sockets for a total of 96 cores running at a base clock rate of 2.3GHz.  Each CPU has the AVX2 capability to provide 256-bit-wide SIMD vector registers."},{"idx":41,"label":"12","overlay":4,"note":"For the CUDA, it’s equipped with a single NVIDIA H100 GPU."},{"idx":42,"label":"12","overlay":5,"note":"It also has a 100Gb Intel Omni-Path node interconnect, which is useful for MPI, although for these cases we did verify that all allocated nodes were in a single cabinet on Viking with reliable network latencies."},{"idx":46,"label":"12","overlay":9,"note":"All variants were built with the IntelLLVM compiler in the 2024 edition of the OneAPI toolchain, in Release mode of course.  We did include debugging symbols in the binaries to make sense of profiling and dynamic analysis reports, but there’s no reason to believe this degraded performance."},{"idx":49,"label":"12","overlay":12,"note":"In all cases, simulations were run for two seconds over a fixed domain size, at varying resolutions.  Of course we also disabled serialisation when assessing performance as this was introducing an unacceptable amount of variance in the results."},{"idx":50,"label":"13","overlay":0,"note":"We’ll now show a series of timing reports and roofline graphs that depict whether our iterative solving algorithms, identified with circles with radii representative of the overall compute, are bound by limits on the rate of memory transactions or compute operations.\n\nWe’ll go through these very quickly, but first for the serial performance, we have a fairly low-resolution grid where the 4-metre dimension was allocated 256 cells and the 1-metre allocated 64 cells.  As we would expect, the SOR computation took the vast majority of the wall clock time."},{"idx":51,"label":"14","overlay":0,"note":"We see this trend continue as we double the resolution."},{"idx":52,"label":"15","overlay":0,"note":"And even further more for the highest resolution.  Clearly by the wall clock times shown, the serial implementation scales very poorly as we effectively increase the problem size."},{"idx":53,"label":"16","overlay":0,"note":"As we move to OpenMP performance, we see a different picture with new bottlenecks identified.  In particular for the lower resolutions, OpenMP runtime calls such as enforced data barriers, where threads were waiting for neighbouring data, and thread despatch calls where POSIX was spinning up new threads from the pool, was significant in relation to the compute by the vectorised solvers.\n\nThe actual compute for the SOR and SOR residual takes a minority of the runtime for these degenerate cases."},{"idx":54,"label":"17","overlay":0,"note":"This theme subsides as we increase the resolution and the compute demands increase.  As we would expect, the initial spawning of threads takes a lesser proportion of the overall time, as does waiting at barriers, as this doesn’t necessarily scale neatly with problem size."},{"idx":55,"label":"18","overlay":0,"note":"Finally with the highest resolution, we see OpenMP thread despatch dominating due to the large number of threads required, and the compute requirements is at an all-time high."},{"idx":56,"label":"19","overlay":0,"note":"Moving onto MPI, of course we don’t have OpenMP calls to concern ourselves with but we do have the OpenMPI runtime.  At these lower resolutions, again the startup and teardown is quite substantial, but as bottlenecks we have halo exchanges and reductions, essentially where explicit message-passing is required over the interconnect."},{"idx":57,"label":"20","overlay":0,"note":"As we increase the resolution further, the SOR computation takes a larger proportion of the time, and the footprint of the message-passing is diminished as we would expect."},{"idx":58,"label":"21","overlay":0,"note":"This is shown most extremely for the highest resolution, where MPI runtime footprint is minimal."},{"idx":59,"label":"22","overlay":0,"note":"Finally for CUDA performance, you’ll note that the quantities here are much smaller because of the substantial advantages of running an embarrassingly parallel problem on a GPU, especially one such as the H100.\n\nWe can see that our main bottlenecks were the allocation of data on the GPU device, copying of data between devices and host, and finally the kernel invocations which encapsulate all compute previously discussed including solving the Poisson PDE and reduction of the Laplacian residual to determine convergence."},{"idx":60,"label":"23","overlay":0,"note":"We can see this trend remain constant.  Memory allocation and transactions scale particularly poorly at higher resolution due to the vastly increased number of cells."},{"idx":62,"label":"25","overlay":0,"note":"Insofar as comparing wall clock runtimes, we have this graph which is not particularly useful in its current form, due to the heavy deviations across the different ports.  We can clearly see that the serial implementation scales extremely poorly as we increase the resolution."},{"idx":63,"label":"26","overlay":0,"note":"But if we remove the serial category we can have a more reasonable survey of the runtimes.  As we would expect, OpenMP, due to only have one core at its disposal is significantly slower.  This is reflected with MPI, when we’re running on Viking we have over 96 single-threaded ranks, so we would expect there to be massively improved performance notwithstanding message-passing."},{"idx":64,"label":"27","overlay":0,"note":"Finally we can take a closer look at the performance of the CUDA implementation compared to CPU-based approaches.  Firstly note that there isn’t really a measurable difference between the two lower resolutions, with only a slight difference becoming apparent on the highest resolution.  As shown in the earlier breakdown acquired with the Nsight Systems tool, this can be attributed to larger memory throughput requirements and not slower kernel execution."},{"idx":65,"label":"28","overlay":0,"note":"So finally, just a discussion on the engineering solutions.  All applications presented herein followed object-oriented SW engineering principles; of course the implementations in C had to sort-of mimic this."},{"idx":66,"label":"28","overlay":1,"note":"It was notably easier to organise the codebase in the CUDA variant due to features inherited from modern C++ (that’s versions 11 through 17 as possibly 20 with the newest compute capabilities)."},{"idx":67,"label":"28","overlay":2,"note":"In terms of lines of code, the OpenMP was smallest as would be expected, coming in at about 1082 lines excluding Doxygen annotations.  MPI was the largest at 1940 due to the increased complexity of domain decomposition, subdomain flagging, and message-passing.  And CUDA was in the middle at 1366."},{"idx":68,"label":"28","overlay":3,"note":"The OpenMP and MPI solutions are highly portable to build and run, noting MPI always uses dynamic linkage, as they’re both making sole use of free, open-source, and extremely well-supported implementations of known standards.  CUDA is however vendor-locked to NVIDIA’s proprietary technology, so is significantly less portable."},{"idx":69,"label":"28","overlay":4,"note":"With that said, all solutions can be subject to dynamic analysis.  Profiling is particularly important here, but common tools such as Valgrind, the Address Sanitizers from Google, and NVIDIA’s cuda-memchk all came in invaluable for verifying correctness of runtime heap behaviour."},{"idx":70,"label":"28","overlay":5,"note":"MPI and CUDA were much more effort to debug interactively due to their distributed topology, however this could be mitigated in most cases by considering smaller cases to eliminate bugs, then scaling up gradually.  Of course considering all the threads on a CUDA device, kernel device code can’t be debugged in a traditional way, but thankfully NVIDIA provides userland utilities to help with this."},{"idx":71,"label":"28","overlay":6,"note":"Finally, the build process and remote workflows on Viking were homogenised with a relatively standard collection of tools."},{"idx":72,"label":"28","overlay":7,"note":"I’ll just mention CMake for generation of Makefiles and resolution of system dependencies, such as the runtimes required for each of the ports."},{"idx":73,"label":"28","overlay":8,"note":"Also CLion from JetBrains for general development work."},{"idx":74,"label":"28","overlay":9,"note":"And LLDB and Cuda-GDB with the remote server debugging capabilities."},{"idx":75,"label":"28","overlay":10,"note":"And finally the purpose-built tools for engineering performance-critical applications, such as all tooling from Intel in the OneAPI userland toolkits, and NVIDIA with Nsight Compute and Nsight Systems."},{"idx":76,"label":"29","overlay":0,"note":"I hope you found this interesting, thank you very much for watching."}]}